{"content":"Investigated and confirmed the root cause: in `FunctionBuilder.get_invocation_str`, the branch that decides whether an argument is forwarded positionally vs. as a keyword had `parts.append(arg)` and `kw_parts.append(arg + '=' + arg)` swapped relative to the documented rules in the comment above (defaulted/kwonly-only-target args should be forwarded as keywords; positional-only args and all args when `*varargs` is present should stay positional). This swap caused argument values to be passed in the wrong order/form when calling the wrapped target, producing errors ranging from `TypeError: got multiple values for argument` to signature-formatting `IndexError: tuple index out of range` (since `get_sig_str`/`inspect_formatargspec` index into `defaults` using positions computed assuming the documented positional/keyword split).

Fixed by swapping the two branches back so defaulted-and-not-varargs/not-posonly args (and args forced by `target_kwonly`) go to `kw_parts`, while posonly args and args that must stay positional (varargs present) go to `parts`. Verified with multiple reproductions: plain defaults, keyword-only args, posonly args (PEP 570), `*args/**kwargs` passthrough with keyword-only args, and `expected=[(name, default)]` usage — all now produce correct results matching the documented semantics. Also reran `pytest --doctest-modules boltons/funcutils.py -q`: still 10 passed.

Note: one of my own exploratory test cases (`expected=['c']` with no explicit default, expecting the generated wrapper to tolerate a missing `c`) still raises `TypeError: missing required positional argument 'c'` — but that is actually correct/intended behavior per the `expected` argument semantics (omitting a default means the arg becomes required in the new signature); it is not a bug.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "
